JA4+ fingerprinting (built into the Caddy image by caddy/Dockerfile)

  github.com/matt-/caddy-ja4
  github.com/lum8rjack/caddy-ja4h

These implement the JA4+ suite originated by FoxIO, LLC. The JA4 method and the
JA4+ specification are distributed under the FoxIO License 1.1, which permits
research and internal use but restricts commercial use, including offering the
fingerprinting capability as part of a paid product or service, without a
licence from FoxIO.

  https://github.com/FoxIO-LLC/ja4/blob/main/LICENSE-JA4
  https://github.com/lum8rjack/caddy-ja4h/blob/main/NOTICE

If you deploy the Caddy image built by caddy/Dockerfile, or redistribute it, the
FoxIO terms apply to you. Running this honeypot as personal or academic research
is within them. Building a commercial product on the fingerprints it collects is
not, absent a licence.

To avoid the dependency entirely, use the fingerprint-free stack:

    docker compose -f docker-compose.nofingerprint.yml up -d --build

That pairs a stock caddy:2.8-alpine image with Caddyfile.nofingerprint. The app
degrades cleanly: `ja4` and `ja4h` are recorded as empty strings and every other
field is unchanged. analyze.py never reads those two fields, so no part of the
shipped report is affected.

Removing the two `--with` lines from caddy/Dockerfile is NOT sufficient on its
own. The default Caddyfile also uses the ja4 listener wrapper and the ja4 and
ja4h_header directives, and a stock Caddy refuses to parse them:

    Error: getting module named 'caddy.listeners.ja4': module not registered

Caddy then exits and nothing is served. Use the two files above instead.

